      "text": "To find the smallest possible value of \\( n \\) that satisfies the given conditions, we need to solve the following system of congruences:\n\\[ n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4} \\]\n\\[ n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nWe start by expressing the first congruence in a general form:\n\\[ n = 4k + 3 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\). Next, we substitute this expression into the second congruence:\n\\[ 4k + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\\[ 4k + 3 \\equiv 4k + 3 - 4 \\equiv 4k - 1 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSo the congruence becomes:\n\\[ 4k - 1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\nAdding 1 to both sides, we get:\n\\[ 4k \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\nTo solve for \\( k \\), we need the multiplicative inverse of 4 modulo 5. The inverse of 4 modulo 5 is a number \\( x \\) such that:\n\\[ 4x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\]\nBy testing values, we find that \\( x = 4 \\) works because:\n\\[ 4 \\cdot 4 = 16 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSo we multiply both sides of the congruence \\( 4k \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\) by 4:\n\\[ k \\equiv 12 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\nThis means that \\( k \\) can be written as:\n\\[ k = 5m + 2 \\]\nfor some integer \\( m \\). Substituting this back into the expression for \\( n \\), we get:\n\\[ n = 4k + 3 = 4(5m + 2) + 3 = 20m + 8 + 3 = 20m + 11 \\]\nSo \\( n \\) can be expressed as:\n\\[ n = 20m + 11 \\]\nWe are asked to find the smallest possible value of \\( n \\) that is greater than 10. Setting \\( m = 0 \\):\n\\[ n = 20 \\cdot 0 + 11 = 11 \\]\nHowever, we need \\( n \\) to be greater than 10, so we try \\( m = 1 \\):\n\\[ n = 20 \\cdot 1 + 11 = 31 \\]\nThus, the smallest possible value of \\( n \\) that satisfies both congruences and is greater than 10 is:\n\\[ \\boxed{31} \\]",
