      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the smallest value of \\( n \\) that satisfies the following conditions:\n1. When Ray climbs 4 steps at a time, there are 3 steps left at the top. This can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}\n   \\]\n2. When Ray climbs 5 steps at a time, there are 2 steps left at the top. This can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n\nWe need to find the smallest \\( n > 10 \\) that satisfies both congruences. Let's start by expressing \\( n \\) in terms of the first congruence:\n\\[\nn = 4k + 3 \\quad \\text{for some integer } k\n\\]\nWe substitute this expression into the second congruence:\n\\[\n4k + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nSimplifying this, we get:\n\\[\n4k + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\implies 4k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5} \\implies 4k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\implies k \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nThis means that \\( k \\) can be written as:\n\\[\nk = 5m + 1 \\quad \\text{for some integer } m\n\\]\nSubstituting \\( k = 5m + 1 \\) back into the expression for \\( n \\), we get:\n\\[\nn = 4(5m + 1) + 3 = 20m + 4 + 3 = 20m + 7\n\\]\nSo, \\( n \\) can be expressed as:\n\\[\nn = 20m + 7\n\\]\nWe need to find the smallest \\( n > 10 \\). For \\( m = 0 \\), we get:\n\\[\nn = 20 \\cdot 0 + 7 = 7\n\\]\nThis is less than 10, so we try \\( m = 1 \\):\n\\[\nn = 20 \\cdot 1 + 7 = 27\n\\]\nThis is greater than 10. Therefore, the smallest possible value of \\( n \\) that satisfies both conditions and is greater than 10 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{27}\n\\]",
