    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the smallest value of \\( n \\) that satisfies the given conditions when Ray climbs the stairs in steps of 4 and 5. The conditions are:\n\n1. When Ray climbs the stairs at 4 steps at a time, there are 3 steps left at the top. This can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}\n   \\]\n2. When Ray climbs the stairs at 5 steps at a time, there are 2 steps left at the top. This can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n\nWe need to find the smallest \\( n \\) that satisfies both congruences. Let's start by expressing \\( n \\) in terms of the first congruence:\n   \\[\n   n = 4k + 3 \\quad \\text{for some integer } k\n   \\]\nWe substitute this expression into the second congruence:\n   \\[\n   4k + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\nSimplifying this, we get:\n   \\[\n   4k + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\implies 4k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5} \\implies 4k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\implies k \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\nThis means that \\( k \\) can be written as:\n   \\[\n   k = 5m + 1 \\quad \\text{for some integer } m\n   \\]\nSubstituting \\( k = 5m + 1 \\) back into the expression for \\( n \\), we get:\n   \\[\n   n = 4(5m + 1) + 3 = 20m + 4 + 3 = 20m + 7\n   \\]\nSo, \\( n \\) can be written as:\n   \\[\n   n = 20m + 7\n   \\]\nWe need the smallest value of \\( n \\) that is greater than 10. Let's find the smallest \\( m \\) such that \\( n > 10 \\):\n   \\[\n   20m + 7 > 10 \\implies 20m > 3 \\implies m > \\frac{3}{20}\n   \\]\nSince \\( m \\) is an integer, the smallest possible value for \\( m \\) is 1. Substituting \\( m = 1 \\) into the expression for \\( n \\), we get:\n   \\[\n   n = 20 \\cdot 1 + 7 = 27\n   \\]\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of \\( n \\) that is greater than 10 is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{27}\n   \\]",
